+---
+type: spec
+task_slug: parse-iso-duration
+status: approved
+created: 2026-10-15
+tags: [timeparse, spec, python, iso-8601, duration, parsing]
+test_framework: pytest
+interview_rounds: 1
+lane: light
+summary: "Add parse_iso_duration: day-and-time ISO-8601 durations to seconds, exported from timeparse"
+---
+
+# SPEC: parse_iso_duration
+
+## 🎯 Intent
+`timeparse` only parses the compact `1h30m` form. Callers receiving ISO-8601 durations (`PT1H30M`) need the same seconds result without writing their own parser. Only the unambiguous day-and-time subset is in scope, because years, months and weeks have no fixed length in seconds.
+
+## 🌅 Outcomes
+`from timeparse import parse_iso_duration` works, and `parse_iso_duration("P1DT2H")` returns `93600`. Any input outside the grammar `P[nD][T[nH][nM][nS]]` raises `ValueError`. `parse_duration` behaves exactly as before.
+
+## 📋 In-Scope Scenarios
+
+### S1: Valid durations
+**Given** a string in the grammar, optionally with surrounding whitespace
+**When** `parse_iso_duration` is called
+**Then** it returns `86400*D + 3600*H + 60*M + S` as an `int`
+**And** the result is exactly type `int` (not float/bool); values may exceed the next unit (`PT90M` → 5400); `PT0S` → 0
+
+### S2: Invalid durations
+**Given** a string outside the grammar (empty `P`, `PT`, out of order, repeated component, `Y`/`W`/month-before-`T`, fraction, sign, lower case, junk, empty string)
+**When** `parse_iso_duration` is called
+**Then** it raises `ValueError`
+
+### S3: Export and non-regression
+**Given** the `timeparse` package
+**When** it is imported
+**Then** `parse_iso_duration` is importable from `timeparse` and listed in `__all__`
+**And** `parse_duration` returns the same results as before for its existing tests
+
+## 🚫 Non-Goals
+- Years, months, weeks, fractions, signs, lower case, alternative ISO forms such as `P0001-01-01`, or any non-ISO input.
+- Changing `parse_duration`, `UNITS`, `format_duration` or the CLI.
+- Non-`str` input handling beyond Python's natural `AttributeError`/`TypeError`.
+
+## ⚠️ Constraints
+| Constraint | Value | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Test framework | `pytest` | Already used in `tests/test_parse.py` |
+| Digits | ASCII `0-9` only | Unicode digits (e.g. Arabic-Indic) must raise `ValueError`, not be silently accepted |
+| Whitespace | `str.strip()` only at the ends; inner whitespace invalid | "surrounding" whitespace only |
+| Compatibility | Existing Python range in `pyproject.toml` | No new dependency (stdlib `re`) |
+
+## 🔒 Irreversible Decisions
+none — additive function; removable before any release. (Public-API category considered: new name only, no existing contract changes.)
+
+## ✅ Verification Criteria
+| Scenario | Verification mode | Test name / manual step |
+|---|---|---|
+| S1 | unit (golden + property) | `test_iso_valid_examples`, `test_iso_component_sum_property` |
+| S2 | unit (golden) | `test_iso_invalid_raises` |
+| S3 | unit | `test_iso_exported`, existing `tests/test_parse.py` |
+
+### AC-001: Valid examples
+Golden table: `P1D`→86400, `PT1H30M`→5400, `P1DT2H`→93600, `PT0S`→0, `PT90M`→5400, `  PT1S\n`→1. Source: ISO-8601 definition (1 D = 86400 s) and the task's stated examples.
+
+### AC-002: Component-sum property
+For all non-negative integers d,h,m,s, `parse_iso_duration` of the canonically rendered string equals `86400*d+3600*h+60*m+s`; independent of the implementation's regex.
+
+### AC-003: Invalid inputs raise
+Golden reject table: `P`, `PT`, `P1H`, `PT1M1H`, `pt1h`, `P1Y`, `P1W`, `P1M`, `PT1.5S`, `-PT1S`, `PT-1S`, `P1DT`, `P1D1D`, `PT1H1H`, `` , `PT1 H`, `PT١S`.
+
+### AC-004: Export
+`timeparse.parse_iso_duration` exists and `"parse_iso_duration" in timeparse.__all__`; `parse_duration("1h30m") == 5400` still.
+
+## ❓ Open Questions
+none
+
+## 🔎 Spec Validation
+Advisory single pass (NEEDS_REVISION, no critical findings). Addressed: machine AC-003 reject table synced with body; AC-004 predicate now also checks callable and `parse_duration` non-regression; AC-001 table adds whitespace and `PT007S`; `int` return type stated in S1 (test should assert `type(result) is int`). Left to the DRI: the new public export and its ValueError/grammar contract are not listed as an irreversible decision, and "removable before any release" cites no release evidence.
+
+## 🔍 Refinement Decisions
+- Step 0 skip not taken: new public function in two files with many edge cases. Lane `light`: 2 files, one session, no irreversible decision.
+- Round 1: no WHAT ambiguity in the ask; defaulted ASCII-only digits, leading zeros allowed (`PT007S`=7), inner whitespace invalid. No question tool available, so no DRI approval recorded.
